1b).LinkedList
  --Linked List is a data structure where each element (node) stores:  1. Data  2. Address (reference) of the next node
    10 -> 20 -> 30 -> null
* Node 1 contains `10` and points to Node 2.
* Node 2 contains `20` and points to Node 3.
* Node 3 contains `30` and points to `null` (end).

✅ Dynamic size (can grow or shrink)
❌ Extra memory needed for storing references

   LinkedList<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();
        list.add(10);
        list.add(20);
        list.add(30);
        System.out.println(list);  //[10, 20, 30]

### Difference Between ArrayList and LinkedList

1. **ArrayList** internally uses a **dynamic array** to store elements.
   **LinkedList** internally uses a **doubly linked list** to store elements.

2. **ArrayList** insertion and deletion are slower because elements need to be shifted in memory.
   **LinkedList** insertion and deletion are faster because no shifting is required.

3. **ArrayList** is better for storing and accessing data.
   **LinkedList** is better for frequent insertion and deletion of data.

4. In **ArrayList**, elements are stored in **contiguous memory locations**.
   In **LinkedList**, elements are stored in **non-contiguous memory locations**.

5. **ArrayList** has a default initial capacity (10).
   **LinkedList** does not have a default capacity; an empty list is created.

1c).Stack
  -- Stack is a data structure that follows LIFO (Last In, First Out).

        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(10);
        stack.push(20);
        stack.push(30)
        System.out.println(stack);      // [10,20,30]
        System.out.println(stack.peek()); // 30
        System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 30
        System.out.println(stack);        // [10,20]

| Method    | Work                     |
| --------- | ------------------------ |
| push()    | Add element              |
| pop()     | Remove top element       |
| peek()    | View top element         |
| isEmpty() | Check stack empty or not |

# 2) Queue
 ---Queue is a data structure that follows **FIFO (First In, First Out)**.
  element insert from rear and remove from front
        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.add(10);
        q.add(20);
        q.add(30);

        System.out.println(q);        // [10, 20, 30]
        System.out.println(q.peek()); // 10
        System.out.println(q.remove()); // 10
        System.out.println(q);        // [20, 30]

In Queue, offer() and poll() are safer alternatives to add() and remove().
| Method     | Purpose              | If Queue is Full/Empty           |
| ---------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `offer()`  | Add element          | Returns `false` if it can't add  |
| `add()`    | Add element          | Throws exception                 |
| `poll()`   | Remove first element | Returns `null` if queue is empty |
| `remove()` | Remove first element | Throws exception                 |
| `peek()`   | View first element   | Returns `null` if empty          |

2a). # PriorityQueue in Java Collection Framework
    ---PriorityQueue is a special type of Queue where elements are processed based on their **priority** rather than insertion order.
    ---By default, the **smallest element has the highest priority**.

        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.offer(30);
        pq.offer(10);
        pq.offer(20);

        System.out.println(pq.peek()); // 10
        System.out.println(pq.poll()); // 10
        System.out.println(pq.peek()); // 20

## Max Heap (Largest Element First)

PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
pq.offer(30);
pq.offer(10);
pq.offer(20);

System.out.println(pq.peek());//30

    ---tc = o(logn) // offer()
    ---tc = o(logn) // poll()
    ---tc = o(1) // peak()


### Deque in Java Collection Framework
 --- Deque stands for **Double Ended Queue**.
 ---Elements can be added and removed from **both front and rear ends**.
`
      Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();
        dq.addFirst(10);
        dq.addLast(20);
        dq.addFirst(5);

        System.out.println(dq);          // [5, 10, 20]
        System.out.println(dq.peekFirst()); // 5
        System.out.println(dq.peekLast());  // 20

        dq.removeFirst();
        System.out.println(dq);          // [10, 20]

## Important Methods
| Method        | Work               |
| ------------- | ------------------ |
| addFirst()    | Add at front       |                             
| addLast()     | Add at rear        |
| removeFirst() | Remove from front  |
| removeLast()  | Remove from rear   |
| peekFirst()   | View front element |
| peekLast()    | View rear element  |

### Quick Memory Trick
Queue  -> One end insert, one end delete
Stack  -> One end insert/delete
Deque  -> Both ends insert/delete

# Set in Java Collection Framework
 *Set** is a collection that **does not allow duplicate elements**.
--If you add the same value multiple times, it will be stored only once.

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
set.add(10);
System.out.println(set);// [10, 20]
for(Integer e:set){
     System.out.print(e+" ");// 10 20
 }
    }

# Types of Set

## 1. HashSet
* No duplicate values
* No insertion order

HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
System.out.println(set); //[20, 10, 30]

## 2. LinkedHashSet
* No duplicate values
* Maintains insertion order

LinkedHashSet<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
System.out.println(set); //[30, 10, 20]

## 3. TreeSet
* No duplicate values
* Stores elements in sorted order

TreeSet<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
System.out.println(set); //[10, 20, 30]

# Common Methods
set.add(10); // Add element  set.remove(10)// Remove element   set.contains(10);// Check element  set.size(); // Number of elements   set.isEmpty();// Check empty

### Quick Memory Trick
HashSet       -> Unique + Unordered
LinkedHashSet -> Unique + Insertion Order
TreeSet       -> Unique + Sorted
| Collection         | add()    | remove() | contains()/search | 
| ------------------ | -------- | -------- | ----------------- | 
| HashSet            | O(1)     | O(1)     | O(1)              |           
| LinkedHashSet      | O(1)     | O(1)     | O(1)              |       
| TreeSet            | O(log n) | O(log n) | O(log n)          |            

















